  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 135 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nSynopsis: High pressure will shift toward Ontario & the Saint \nLawrence Valley Monday afternoon & evening, allowing southerly winds \n& warm/moist advection to commence areawide. An upper trough will \nconcurrently advance toward the Lake Superior vicinity, with \nattendant surface cyclogenesis near Lake Winnipeg further \nreinforcing the southerly return flow & warm advection. The \ndeveloping surface low will pull a cold front into the western Great \nLakes Monday night into Tuesday, with the boundary stalling near or \nsouth of the Wisconsin-Illinois border through the mid-week time \nframe. Between the amplifying warm/moist advection & encroaching \nsurface front, additional periods of showers and storms are thus \nexpected from Monday night into at least Tuesday. Whether or not \nprecip chances will linger into Wednesday will depend on the \nstalling location of the passing surface front, with a number of \ndifferent solutions apparent in current forecast guidance. Some \nstorms---particularly those occurring Monday night & Tuesday---could \nbe stronger with locally heavy rainfall. The next area of high \npressure will arrive by Thursday, bringing quieter and seasonable \nconditions for the end of next week.\n\nMonday Evening & Night: Shower and storm chances increase areawide \ngiven an abundance of arriving large scale forcing. Given the \nsynoptics in play, confidence is high in the occurrence of \nwidespread showers and storms, with the 50-80%+ precip probabilities \nfrom current NBM guidance appearing more than reasonable. What \nremains less certain is the hazards potential---particularly locally \nheavy rainfall & damaging wind gusts---that will accompany the \nexpected storms. Materialization of hazards will reside in the \nplacement & arrival timing of the unseasonably moist & modestly \nunstable air mass being ushered in by southerly return flow. \nAbundant deep layer shear on the order of 40+ kts will overlap with \nthis air mass, supporting at least some potential for organized \nstorms capable of gusty winds. How far this air mass gets & how \nquickly it does so will depend on the relative strengths of \ndeparting high pressure & the surface low forming near Lake \nWinnipeg. Any combination of stronger high pressure and/or weaker \nlow pressure would result in a slower-arriving favorable air mass \nconfined to more western parts of the area. Opposite combinations of \nhigh & low strengths would conversely allow favorable thermodynamics \nto arrive over more of southern Wisconsin in shorter order. Air mass \nrecovery & its relation to evolving surface features will thus be \nthe primary trend to monitor into this weekend. Current forecast \nthinking favors that of a more western solution, with the \nunderstanding that additional shifts are possible as additional \nforecast guidance arrives through the weekend. Will continue to \nprovide updates in coming forecasts.  \n\nTuesday: Depending on where the primary cold front ends up in the \nwake of Monday night precip, could see more shower & storm \ndevelopment with additional locally heavy rainfall potential. Have \ntrended likely (~60+%) precip probabilities toward the currently-\nfavored position of the cold frontal boundary, which would place \nareas near/south of I-94 & US-18 in line for the best chances of \nadditional storm development. Anticipate additional shifts in \npreferred corridors for storm redevelopment, with trends being \nmonitored over coming updates.\n\nQuigley",
